WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:22.071
“He was a 
bad son, a bad husband, and a bad king, but

00:00:22.071 --> 00:00:24.789
a gallant and splendid soldier.”

00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:29.410
That was the description that historian Sir
Steven Runciman thought best fitted Richard

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:30.410
I.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:35.430
This was in line with the criticism brought
against Richard by Victorian scholar William

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:41.620
Stubbs who considered him a “mere warrior”
who had no care for his kingdom or sympathy

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:42.830
for its people.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:48.760
Indeed, Richard I was an absentee ruler, spending
only a combined six months out of his ten-year

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:49.760
reign in England.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.620
The rest of the time, the king was either
involved in wars or in captivity or simply

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.730
preferring to reside in his French territories
such as Anjou and Aquitaine.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.710
Today, scholars debate whether the king even
knew how to speak English or not as he preferred

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.120
his mother’s language of Occitan.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.000
And yet, English history remembers him quite
fondly.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.890
He was, after all, Richard the Lionheart,
but was he truly deserving of this moniker?

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:22.080
We will let you decide that for yourself as
we explore the life of King Richard I.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:29.170
Early Years
Richard was born on September 8, 1157, in

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.170
Oxford, England.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:35.530
He was the son of Henry II, King of England,
and Eleanor of Aquitaine, a very powerful

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:40.310
and influential woman in her own right who
prior to her marriage to Henry also served

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.800
as Queen of the Franks as she had been the
wife of King Louis VII before their marriage

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.100
was annulled.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.700
Richard was part of the House of Plantagenet,
a royal dynasty that ruled over England for

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.880
approximately 330 years.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.090
His grandfather, in fact, Geoffrey V, Count
of Anjou, was the one who founded the house,

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.760
while his father became the first Plantagenet
to claim the English throne.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.920
It was generally not expected that Richard
would become king as he was the third son

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.369
of Henry and Eleanor.

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:12.030
The eldest son, William, died young, before
Richard was even born.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.430
His other older sibling, though, Henry, was
the one ordained to follow in his father’s

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:17.430
footsteps.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:23.040
In fact, in 1170, the family made an unusual
move for English royalty and crowned the 15-year-old

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.100
Henry as titular King of England while his
father was still alive.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.920
He became known as Henry the Young King to
help distinguish him.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.420
This coronation was in name only and the elder
Henry still retained authority.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.250
This became a bone of contention between father
and son as the Young Henry grew frustrated

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.640
with his father giving away possessions he
considered to be rightfully his.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.940
He was spurred on by other noblemen, both
English and foreign, who wanted to get rid

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.530
of the elder king, and even his own mother
who had a falling out with her husband.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:57.070
This led to the Revolt of 1173-1174.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:02.230
Young King Henry had powerful allies including
the aforementioned King Louis VII and William

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.870
the Lion, King of Scots.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:09.270
He almost managed to secure victory but, in
the end, Henry II was triumphant.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.830
What role did Richard play in all of this?

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:16.050
By the time of the uprising, he had already
been made Duke of Aquitaine, a region of modern

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.099
France which, back then, was a duchy that
became an English possession when Eleanor

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:22.260
married Henry.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.390
It was also the place where Richard spent
a lot of his time both as a child and as an

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.270
adult and which, by all accounts, he seemed
to cherish far more than the whole of England.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:36.610
Richard actually sided with his sibling during
the revolt, as did their younger brother,

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.610
Geoffrey.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:42.300
Of course, he was still a young man at that
point and was clearly being influenced by

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.709
other people, most likely his mother and King
Louis of France.

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.900
In fact, after the rebellion had been put
down, Henry II imprisoned Eleanor of Aquitaine

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.670
to keep the boys in line and to minimize her
influence on them.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.600
She would stay under lock and key for the
next 16 years.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:09.269
Richard the Duke
As for his rebellious sons, Henry was merciful

00:04:09.269 --> 00:04:12.129
and forgave them after they submitted to him.

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:17.019
Richard retained the title of Duke of Aquitaine
and his father tasked him with the punishment

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:19.919
of the nobles who rebelled against the English
king.

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:23.550
Henry’s orders were for most castles in
the duchy to be reduced to the state they

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:28.229
were 15 days before the start of the uprising,
but he specified that some of them should

00:04:28.229 --> 00:04:30.629
be completely razed to the ground.

00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:35.029
Obviously, this did not sit well with some
of the local noblemen who refused to stand

00:04:35.029 --> 00:04:38.330
down and continued their rebellion against
Richard.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:43.789
Henry placed a lot of trust in his 18-year-old
son and his military prowess as he gave Richard

00:04:43.789 --> 00:04:48.069
full command over the duchy’s armies, as
well as control over local revenues.

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:52.529
Perhaps he was impressed with how Richard
handled himself during the revolt, even if

00:04:52.529 --> 00:04:53.849
it was against him.

00:04:53.849 --> 00:04:57.979
Certainly, Richard would develop a reputation
as a great fighter and military leader, as

00:04:57.979 --> 00:05:02.720
opposed to his older brother Henry who was
described as giving “no evidence of political

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.819
sagacity, military skill, or even ordinary
intelligence.”

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:12.669
Richard spent the next few years as his father’s
regent in Aquitaine, putting down rebellions.

00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:18.699
We have knowledge of his efforts courtesy
of 12th century chronicler Roger of Hoveden.The

00:05:18.699 --> 00:05:23.819
young duke made special mention of his successful
siege of Castillon-sur-Agen.

00:05:23.819 --> 00:05:28.439
It was heavily fortified and required the
use of artillery but, after two months, Richard

00:05:28.439 --> 00:05:30.040
managed to break the garrison.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.409
Clearly, he was quite pleased with himself
as it was, arguably, his first impressive

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:35.569
military triumph.

00:05:35.569 --> 00:05:40.740
These successes started establishing his reputation
as an adept commander, but also his penchant

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.990
for cruelty as he showed little mercy to those
who stood in his way.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.810
Some nobles proved to be bigger challenges
than others.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.159
The Rancon family, for instance, were a major
thorn in Richard’s side.

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:57.159
They were lords in Taillebourg, led by Geoffrey
de Rancon, and their castle was considered

00:05:57.159 --> 00:05:58.159
impregnable.

00:05:58.159 --> 00:06:02.249
Built on a rocky outcrop on the right bank
of the Charente River, it was unassailable

00:06:02.249 --> 00:06:05.349
from three sides while the fourth was heavily
fortified.

00:06:05.349 --> 00:06:09.849
Richard’s strategy was to make himself an
irresistible target so that his enemies would

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:11.599
leave the fortress and come to him.

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:14.319
He destroyed the village that was nestled
beneath the citadel.

00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:16.520
He began burning down the surrounding fields.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:21.090
He even placed his camp close enough to the
castle walls so that Rancon would consider

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.159
a surprise attack.

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:27.559
Meanwhile, he deployed artillery to make it
look like a standard siege and then waited.

00:06:27.559 --> 00:06:31.999
Sure enough, his enemies launched a counterattack,
but were defeated by the duke’s army.

00:06:31.999 --> 00:06:35.909
Richard’s men were then able to force their
way into the castle and, after three days

00:06:35.909 --> 00:06:38.830
of looting and destruction, Rancon surrendered.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:43.249
This was truly a notable victory so much so
that, upon hearing of it, other rebellious

00:06:43.249 --> 00:06:48.099
noblemen decided that the fight was not worth
it and also laid down their arms in front

00:06:48.099 --> 00:06:50.189
of the Duke of Aquitaine.

00:06:50.189 --> 00:06:58.009
Rise to the Throne
Peace throughout the land did not last long.

00:06:58.009 --> 00:07:02.419
Many historians of that time all said that
Richard was a cruel man who “oppressed his

00:07:02.419 --> 00:07:05.919
subjects with unjustified demands and a regime
of violence.”

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:10.849
All the nobles hated him so they banded together
to try and drive him away from the region

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:14.210
and even sought the help of Philip II of France.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:17.219
Eventually, King Henry had to intervene and
he ordered Henry the Younger to come along,

00:07:17.219 --> 00:07:18.219
as well.

00:07:18.219 --> 00:07:19.219
He listened to the local noblemen.

00:07:19.219 --> 00:07:22.610
Again, Roger of Hoveden gave the most detailed
account, saying that the barons complained

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:27.619
that Richard took their wives and daughters
by force to satiate his own lust and that

00:07:27.619 --> 00:07:30.270
when he was done with them, he gave them to
his soldiers.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:34.930
We don’t know if such accusations were true,
but it didn’t matter because Henry saw nothing

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:35.960
wrong with them.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:38.559
Like father, like son, I suppose.

00:07:38.559 --> 00:07:41.729
He subdued the rebellion in 1182 and returned
to England.

00:07:41.729 --> 00:07:44.030
The nobles saw another opportunity, though.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:48.369
While Richard and King Henry could not be
reasoned with, perhaps they could appeal to

00:07:48.369 --> 00:07:49.879
Henry the Younger.

00:07:49.879 --> 00:07:54.339
Almost a decade after his failed revolt had
passed and he still had no lands to claim

00:07:54.339 --> 00:07:58.680
as his own while Geoffrey had Brittany and
Richard had Aquitaine.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.439
Of course, in time, when his father died,
he would have claimed the whole of England,

00:08:02.439 --> 00:08:04.400
but Henry was impatient.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.279
When the French barons promised they would
recognize him as the new duke, the idea of

00:08:09.279 --> 00:08:12.270
invading Aquitaine was firmly implanted in
his head.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.380
All he needed was an excuse that would appease
his father.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:20.099
That excuse came in the form of a castle which
sat on the border between the lands of Richard

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:24.520
and King Henry, a castle that Richard had
spent time repairing and reinforcing.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:28.909
Young Henry wanted it and the king ordered
the Duke of Aquitaine to give it to his brother

00:08:28.909 --> 00:08:30.330
as homage.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:35.930
Richard refused and his brother invaded his
land with help from many Aquitaine nobles.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:38.940
Younger brother Geoffrey also joined the invasion
against Richard.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:43.830
Although at first he tried to remain neutral,
the elder King Henry eventually sided with

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:44.830
Richard.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:47.310
Even so, the war was not going in their favor.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:53.370
Henry the Young King was poised to win, but
he fell ill suddenly and, on June 11, 1183,

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.780
he died of dysentery, marking the end of the
rebellion.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.840
Richard was now heir to the throne.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:04.680
His other brother Geoffrey also died in 1186,
allegedly by being trampled during a jousting

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:05.680
tournament.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.970
There was only one other brother remaining
- the youngest of all, John, who, so far,

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:15.060
had not taken part in any of his family’s
political and military machinations.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.260
Contemporary chroniclers claimed that John
was his father’s favorite.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:23.260
Not very hard to imagine seeing as how he
was the only legitimate son of King Henry

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.650
who didn’t raise his sword against him.

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:30.070
Because Richard was now poised to rule over
England and other territories such as Anjou

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:34.390
and Normandy, King Henry wanted him to give
the duchy of Aquitaine to John.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:39.810
Richard refused as Aquitaine was the territory
he cared about most, perhaps even the only

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:40.970
one he truly cared about.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:45.550
For a while, it looked like it would be war
again between the Plantagenets, but Henry

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:47.190
had an ace up his sleeve.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:50.690
He finally released his wife, Eleanor of Aquitaine,
from prison.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:55.650
She returned to her homeland where she still
was the rightful ruler and Richard, who still

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:59.100
had great affection for his mother, willingly
surrendered all power.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:04.830
However, he found a new ally in King Philip
II of France, also known as Philip Augustus.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:09.610
There were tensions between Henry and Philip
over a marriage that fell through and they

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:11.060
escalated to violence.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:15.340
Richard officially paid homage to Philip and
sided with him against his father.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:20.560
The duo were triumphant and, at the same time,
the older Henry fell gravely ill.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.390
After he was defeated, Henry was forced to
recognize Richard as sole heir.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:11:07.020
He died two days later, on July 6, 1189, and
Richard became the new King

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:25.660
of England.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:30.100
Richard may have been the new king, but he
had no time to concern himself with matters

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.890
such as running his kingdom - he had a crusade
to get to.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:40.210
Ever since 1187 when Sultan Saladin, founder
of the Ayyubid Dynasty, had captured Jerusalem,

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:44.710
the pope had been calling on the European
powers to launch a new crusade and take back

00:11:44.710 --> 00:11:45.710
the Holy Land.

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:49.820
And by the way, if you would like to study
the topic in-depth and see it from the other

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:55.740
perspective, we have already done a Biographics
video on Saladin, you can check that out in

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:57.000
the link below.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.380
There were three major powers involved in
the crusade: England and France, led by Richard

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:06.700
and Philip, and the Holy Roman Empire, led
by Frederick I or Frederick Barbarossa.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:12.040
There were, of course, vassals, such as the
Hungarian forces led by Prince Géza and the

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:17.000
Austrians led by Duke Leopold V.
King Henry II was actually the one who pledged

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.600
his service to the crusade, or “taken the
cross”, as it was called, but he wasn’t

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.770
exactly enthusiastic about the cause.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:28.480
He was old, and frail, and had plenty of things
back home to worry about, but public opinion

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.660
eventually pressured him into joining.

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:34.560
There were actually preachers and minstrels
back then who would walk into villages or

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:38.100
town squares and rally the people to take
the cross.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:42.850
They used propaganda pictures such as images
of Christ being struck by an Arab soldier

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:48.040
or of his tomb being trampled by a Saracen
knight and his horse peeing on it.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:53.360
Richard needed no such persuasion nor was
he doing it out of obligation like his father.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:59.250
We cannot say how devout he truly was, but
he was a soldier looking for a fight and,

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:05.420
back then, there was no greater fight than
taking down Saladin and recapturing Jerusalem.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:10.020
He did apparently have some kind of religious
experience en route to the Holy Land and became

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:14.410
overcome by a need to confess his sins and
renounce his wickedness.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:18.460
He summoned all the bishops, stripped himself
naked, kneeled in front of them and began

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:20.440
flailing himself with whips.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:24.860
Before all this, though, Richard needed money
to raise and equip an army for his crusade.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:31.040
Prior to his death, his father enacted a new
tax called the Aid of 1188, or better known

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:34.630
as the Saladin tithe, as did Philip in France.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:39.820
King Richard also began selling official positions
and other privileges, while also forcing those

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.810
who already held such titles to pay a tidy
sum in order to keep them.

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:48.560
As you can imagine, this did not sit well
with many of his countrymen, both rich and

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:49.560
poor.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:53.400
Richard had little concern for them, though,
as it was often said that he looked at England

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.870
not as a kingdom, but as a source of money
and soldiers.

00:13:56.870 --> 00:14:00.860
According to legend, when someone complained
about the king’s new money-making schemes,

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:07.870
he declared that he would sell London itself
if he could find a buyer.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:11.400
Marriage Troubles

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:16.029
Richard left for the crusade in the summer
of 1190, accompanied by Philip Augustus who

00:14:16.029 --> 00:14:17.070
led the French army.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:21.860
His first stop was in Sicily where Richard
sought to resolve an unrelated matter.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:27.040
The previous year, King William the Good of
Sicily had died and his cousin Tancred became

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:28.040
the new king.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.730
Tancred also imprisoned William’s widow
and denied her inheritance.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:35.570
As it happened, that widow, Queen Joan of
Sicily, was Richard’s sister.

00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:39.620
Unsurprisingly, the English king demanded
that his sister would be released and given

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:40.620
her inheritance.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:45.200
Tancred refused so Richard captured and plundered
the capital of Messina.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:49.640
Eventually, the two kings signed a treaty
and this was mostly notable because it prompted

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:51.590
Richard to assign an heir.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:56.060
It wasn’t his younger brother John, but
Arthur of Brittany, the only son of his dead

00:14:56.060 --> 00:15:00.250
brother Geoffrey, who would have to marry
one of King Tancred’s daughters.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:05.529
Speaking of marriages, Richard himself needed
a queen and, while he was in Sicily, his betrothed

00:15:05.529 --> 00:15:06.529
arrived.

00:15:06.529 --> 00:15:10.430
She was Berengaria, daughter of King Sancho
the Wise of Navarre.

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:12.390
This caused problems with his French ally.

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:16.880
You might recall we previously mentioned that
King Philip of France and King Henry II of

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.630
England had a falling out over a failed marriage.

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:24.470
That marriage was supposed to be between Richard
and Philip’s half-sister, Alys.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:29.540
It had been arranged while the two were children
and Alys came to live at Henry’s court until

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:30.820
she came of age.

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:33.400
However, both Henry and Richard kept delaying
the wedding.

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:38.430
According to sordid rumors, Henry had taken
Alys as a mistress and Richard knew this and

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.370
did not want to marry her anymore.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:44.470
Philip and Richard managed to reach a settlement
to finally break off the marriage, but the

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.920
two left Sicily at different times.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.470
Richard set off with his fleet across the
Mediterranean in April 1191.

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:54.170
However, he encountered a great storm which
destroyed some of his ships.

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:58.620
The vessel which carried his sister and his
wife-to-be shipwrecked on Cyprus where they

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:03.150
were taken prisoner by Isaac Komnenos, the
tyrant who ruled over the island.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:08.040
Richard successfully secured the release of
Joan and Berengaria, but Komnenos refused

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:11.779
to return the large treasure which had also
been aboard the ship.

00:16:11.779 --> 00:16:15.470
Obviously, he was feeling confident in his
position, but fate was against him.

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:17.690
Richard invaded Cyprus and conquered it.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:21.250
Not only did he get his treasure back plus
all the stuff he looted on top of it, but

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:25.990
he later also sold Cyprus to Guy of Lusignan,
the former King of Jerusalem.

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:30.900
While on Cyprus, Richard married Berengaria
and the new queen returned to her native home.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:34.811
She became known as “the only English queen
never to set foot in the country” because

00:16:34.811 --> 00:16:38.990
it was believed she never visited England,
although it is possible she may have done

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:40.560
it after Richard’s death.

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:45.360
The two never had any children, although Richard
did father an illegitimate child named Philip

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:49.710
of Cognac whose mother remains a mystery.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:56.590
The War for Jerusalem
After all the detours and distractions were

00:16:56.590 --> 00:17:01.770
finished, Richard finally arrived in the Holy
Land in the summer of 1191.

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:06.009
The port city of Acre was the first target
of the crusaders and it fell pretty easily.

00:17:06.009 --> 00:17:11.429
In fact, Richard’s main problem during this
crusade were not the enemies, but rather his

00:17:11.429 --> 00:17:12.429
allies.

00:17:12.429 --> 00:17:16.180
He and Philip were already on the outs, not
only over what had happened previously, but

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:19.360
also because the French king wanted half of
Cyprus.

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:24.169
As Philip fell ill, he decided to return home
after the fall of Acre.

00:17:24.169 --> 00:17:27.220
Then there was the Holy Roman Empire and its
vassals.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:31.490
The king, Frederick Barbarossa, had actually
already died by this point in not quite the

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:33.130
most dignified way, to be honest.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:37.679
He didn’t perish in glorious battle, but
rather he drowned crossing the Saleph River

00:17:37.679 --> 00:17:39.179
in modern-day Turkey.

00:17:39.179 --> 00:17:43.370
Some sources say that the old king tried to
brave the currents single-handedly, but others

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:47.950
specify that he was thrown off his horse and
he was too weak and his armor too heavy to

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:48.950
get up again.

00:17:48.950 --> 00:18:01.169
Either way, his death caused many German soldiers
to return home and left Duke Leopold of Austria

00:18:01.169 --> 00:18:05.429
in charge.

00:18:05.429 --> 00:18:09.789
Leopold and Richard did not get along and
the latter greatly insulted the former after

00:18:09.789 --> 00:18:14.259
the duke hanged his banner in triumph alongside
the English and French ones after the battle

00:18:14.259 --> 00:18:15.259
at Acre.

00:18:15.259 --> 00:18:20.190
Richard felt that, as a vassal, Leopold was
being arrogant, so he had the Austrian banner

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:21.190
torn down.

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:24.960
In reply, the Duke of Austria took his men
and went home.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:26.740
The crusade lasted for another year.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:31.090
Richard had other successes, particularly
at the Battle of Arsuf where he scored a decisive

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:35.309
victory despite being taken by surprise and
being heavily outnumbered.

00:18:35.309 --> 00:18:40.470
This was, perhaps, Richard’s greatest triumph
and it allowed him to retake the city of Jaffa.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:45.299
Richard now had the upper hand against Saladin
but, again, he was facing troubles from his

00:18:45.299 --> 00:18:46.299
own camp.

00:18:46.299 --> 00:18:50.490
Many of the men grew tired of the fight, and
the French soldiers that Philip left behind

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.909
simply refused to obey him any longer.

00:18:52.909 --> 00:18:56.889
Furthermore, disturbing reports from back
home said that his brother John was making

00:18:56.889 --> 00:18:58.220
a play for the throne.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.929
To top it all off, Richard fell ill with scurvy.

00:19:00.929 --> 00:19:04.889
Perhaps for the first time in his life, the
war-obsessed king acted practical.

00:19:04.889 --> 00:19:09.149
He concluded that he would not be able to
keep Jerusalem, even if, by some miracle,

00:19:09.149 --> 00:19:12.129
he managed to capture it, so he opened peace
talks.

00:19:12.129 --> 00:19:17.669
On September 2, 1192, Richard and Saladin
signed the Treaty at Jaffa.

00:19:17.669 --> 00:19:21.669
The Christian crusaders didn’t get much,
but it was the best they could hope for under

00:19:21.669 --> 00:19:22.669
the circumstances.

00:19:22.669 --> 00:19:26.499
Obviously, their main objective of reclaiming
Jerusalem had failed.

00:19:26.499 --> 00:19:30.869
But the treaty guaranteed a three-year truce
between both sides, allowed the crusaders

00:19:30.869 --> 00:19:37.230
to keep Acre plus a small part of the coastline
and promised safe passage for Christian pilgrims.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:45.539
The Lion Slain by an Ant
Richard needed to return to England, but was

00:19:45.539 --> 00:19:50.830
wary of traveling through Philip’s territories
as, by that point, the two were openly hostile

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:51.830
to one another.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:55.710
He sailed on the Adriatic, but his ship washed
ashore in Venice.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:59.970
While passing through Vienna, his retinue
was discovered and captured by Duke Leopold

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:02.840
who also had plenty of enmity for the English
king.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:07.950
He turned him over to the new Holy Roman Emperor
Henry VI who imprisoned Richard and asked

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:10.590
for a humongous ransom for his release.

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:15.009
John, of course, had no interest in securing
his brother’s release, but his mother Eleanor

00:20:15.009 --> 00:20:17.080
started immediately to raise the money.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:21.730
She did this, of course, by taxing everyone
to the bone and confiscating treasures and

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:23.010
property.

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:27.029
After over a year in prison, Richard was released
in 1194.

00:20:27.029 --> 00:20:30.929
He returned to England where he was crowned
again to further establish him as the king.

00:20:30.929 --> 00:20:35.120
In a rather strange move, Richard not only
forgave his brother John for revolting, but

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:37.330
also named him as his new heir.

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:42.220
Perhaps Richard simply wanted to calm things
down in England so he could refocus his attention.

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:46.429
He was already ready to go on the warpath
again, this time against France.

00:20:46.429 --> 00:20:50.860
During his incarceration, Philip had conquered
Normandy and Richard wanted to get it back.

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:55.140
King Richard left England less than a month
after his second coronation, never to return

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:56.140
again.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:59.840
He reigned for another five years, but he
spent all that time warring with Philip, only

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:01.840
interrupted by the occasional truce.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:06.110
Richard’s crowning achievement at that time
was the construction of Château Gaillard,

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:10.129
a new castle which was considered a masterpiece
ahead of its time.

00:21:10.129 --> 00:21:14.440
Perhaps fitting for a man so obsessed with
warfare, King Richard’s demise did not come

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:20.269
against a mighty foe such as Saladin or Philip,
but rather while fighting some French peasants.

00:21:20.269 --> 00:21:25.210
He was suppressing a minor revolt in the region
called Limousin and had besieged a small,

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:28.210
unassuming castle named Châlus-Chabrol.

00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:32.960
Richard was patrolling the perimeter and inspecting
his men, paying little notice to the arrows

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:37.830
occasionally fired at them from the castle
walls, even mocking the archers at times.

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:41.990
Fate does not like to be tempted like that
so, of course, a bolt from a crossbow hit

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:44.519
the king in the shoulder, right below the
neck.

00:21:44.519 --> 00:21:47.029
Richard was taken to his tent where he collapsed.

00:21:47.029 --> 00:21:50.070
The wound was poorly treated and developed
gangrene.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:54.240
According to the chronicles of Roger of Hoveden,
the shooter was a boy named Bertrand whose

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:56.440
father and brothers had been killed by Richard.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:01.210
The king allegedly showed mercy and ordered
that the boy be set free and given 100 shillings.

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:05.750
It is unclear if his order was followed, though,
as some sources claim the king’s officers

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:08.850
hanged Bertrand alongside everyone else in
the castle.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:13.590
King Richard died two weeks later on April
6, 1199, by his mother’s side.

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:34.690
As mentioned in his epitaph, “the lion was
slain by the ant.”

